From 786679c0f6c8aafc63f879bcef0dd7874f1671a0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dimitry B <dimitryB@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 18:21:59 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] eip155: search after any failed send no node answered, within
 the budget

The lookup still reported a mined settlement as failed in three ways.

A fast failure got one lookup. A gateway can forward the transaction
and answer 5xx, or drop the connection, in under 10 s, and that is no
more a rejection than a timeout is. The next RPC then rejects the copy,
and the fallback layer reports only that, so the failure is hidden.
Only a node's rejection of something other than a duplicate now skips
the search. "already known" and "nonce too low" are read from the error
text; a node that words them otherwise loses only the search.

The 3 s lookup limit hid a transaction from RPCs that answer in more
than 3 s but under the 10 s request limit. The limit is now 8 s.

The search did not count against the send's budget. It now ends with
the budget. The budget is a target, not a limit: reads and lookups that
have started run to their own limits, so with every RPC slow a settle
can still pass the SDK's 90 s. The hash is then in the log.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
